## The point that must be made

> **Client-side route guards are not security.**

The entire bundle is on the user's machine. They can open DevTools, modify state, and render any component. What stops them is that the API returns 401 or 403 when the data is requested.

A good answer says this without being prompted:

> "The guard is there so users are not shown a broken page. Authorisation is enforced server-side on every endpoint — the client check is UX, not a control."

Getting this wrong is a genuine red flag in an interview.

---

## Where the token should live

| Storage | Verdict |
|---------|---------|
| `httpOnly` cookie | ✅ JavaScript cannot read it, so XSS cannot steal it |
| `localStorage` | ❌ Any script on the page can read it |
| Memory only | ✅ Safest, but lost on refresh |

The common production pattern is a short-lived access token in memory plus a refresh token in an `httpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` cookie.

